The table `customer_analysis_fixed` does not currently exist.

Next, I will analyze the problem query to identify the root causes of issues:

1. The query joins rentals and payments multiple times, but the note says the payment's customer_id may differ from the rental's customer_id. Payments must be aggregated by rental_id first to correctly attribute revenue to the rental's customer.

2. The query counts rentals and sums payments directly from joins, which may cause overcounting if there are multiple payments per rental or multiple categories/actors per rental.

3. The rental counts should only count rentals that have payments, but it seems counting rentals on join without distinct or filtering may overcount.

4. Similarly, categories and actors are counted joining multiple tables without distinct rentals or before filtering properly.

5. Average rental duration calculation uses EXTRACT(days FROM (r.return_date - r.rental_date)) - but this is a float and we want to make sure it's calculated properly on distinct rentals.

Plan to fix:

- Aggregate payment amounts per rental_id first.

- Join rentals with payment aggregates to get only paid rentals.

- Join rentals with customers and other tables using only paid rentals.

- Calculate counts and sums on distinct rentals/payments.

- Filter customers with at least 15 paid rentals.

- Join categories and actors by customer and count only paid rentals.

- Calculate regional popular films similarly using only paid rentals.

I will now rewrite the query with these fixes and then create the table with the fixed results.
| execute_sql {"sql": "WITH paid_rentals AS (\n    SELECT \n        r.rental_id,\n        r.customer_id,\n        r.rental_date,\n        r.return_date,\n        r.inventory_id,\n        SUM(p.amount) AS total_payment\n    FROM rental r\n    JOIN payment p ON r.rental_id = p.rental_id\n    GROUP BY r.rental_id, r.customer_id, r.rental_date, r.return_date, r.inventory_id\n),\n\ncustomer_basic_stats AS (\n    SELECT \n        c.customer_id,\n        c.first_name || ' ' || c.last_name AS customer_name,\n        ci.city AS customer_city,\n        co.country AS customer_country,\n        COUNT(pr.rental_id) AS total_rentals,\n        COUNT(DISTINCT i.film_id) AS unique_films,\n        SUM(pr.total_payment) AS total_spent,\n        AVG(EXTRACT(EPOCH FROM (pr.return_date - pr.rental_date)) / 86400) AS avg_rental_duration\n    FROM customer c\n    JOIN address a ON c.address_id = a.address_id\n    JOIN city ci ON a.city_id = ci.city_id\n    JOIN country co ON ci.country_id = co.country_id\n    JOIN paid_rentals pr ON c.customer_id = pr.customer_id\n    JOIN inventory i ON pr.inventory_id = i.inventory_id\n    WHERE c.email IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name, c.last_name, ci.city, co.country\n    HAVING COUNT(pr.rental_id) >= 15\n),\n\ncustomer_categories AS (\n    SELECT \n        c.customer_id,\n        cat.name AS category_name,\n        COUNT(*) AS category_count,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY c.customer_id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, cat.name ASC) AS rn\n    FROM customer c\n    JOIN paid_rentals pr ON c.customer_id = pr.customer_id\n    JOIN inventory i ON pr.inventory_id = i.inventory_id\n    JOIN film f ON i.film_id = f.film_id\n    JOIN film_category fc ON f.film_id = fc.film_id\n    JOIN category cat ON fc.category_id = cat.category_id\n    WHERE c.email IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY c.customer_id, cat.name\n),\n\ncustomer_actors AS (\n    SELECT \n        c.customer_id,\n        a.first_name || ' ' || a.last_name AS actor_name,\n        COUNT(*) AS actor_count,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY c.customer_id ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, (a.first_name || ' ' || a.last_name) ASC) AS rn\n    FROM customer c\n    JOIN paid_rentals pr ON c.customer_id = pr.customer_id\n    JOIN inventory i ON pr.inventory_id = i.inventory_id\n    JOIN film f ON i.film_id = f.film_id\n    JOIN film_actor fa ON f.film_id = fa.film_id\n    JOIN actor a ON fa.actor_id = a.actor_id\n    WHERE c.email IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY c.customer_id, a.first_name, a.last_name\n),\n\nregional_popular_films AS (\n    SELECT \n        co.country,\n        f.title,\n        COUNT(*) AS rental_count,\n        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY co.country ORDER BY COUNT(*) DESC, f.title ASC) AS rn\n    FROM paid_rentals pr\n    JOIN inventory i ON pr.inventory_id = i.inventory_id\n    JOIN film f ON i.film_id = f.film_id\n    JOIN customer c ON pr.customer_id = c.customer_id\n    JOIN address a ON c.address_id = a.address_id\n    JOIN city ci ON a.city_id = ci.city_id\n    JOIN country co ON ci.country_id = co.country_id\n    WHERE c.email IS NOT NULL\n    GROUP BY co.country, f.title\n)\n\nCREATE TABLE public.customer_analysis_fixed AS\nSELECT \n    cbs.customer_id,\n    cbs.customer_name,\n    cbs.customer_city,\n    cbs.customer_country,\n    cbs.total_rentals,\n    cbs.unique_films,\n    cbs.total_spent,\n    cc.category_name AS favorite_category,\n    ca.actor_name AS favorite_actor,\n    cbs.avg_rental_duration,\n    CASE \n        WHEN cbs.total_spent >= 150 THEN 'Premium'\n        WHEN cbs.total_spent >= 75 THEN 'Standard'\n        ELSE 'Basic'\n    END AS customer_tier,\n    rpf.title AS most_popular_film_in_region,\n    rpf.rental_count AS regional_film_rental_count\nFROM customer_basic_stats cbs\nLEFT JOIN customer_categories cc ON cbs.customer_id = cc.customer_id AND cc.rn = 1\nLEFT JOIN customer_actors ca ON cbs.customer_id = ca.customer_id AND ca.rn = 1\nLEFT JOIN regional_popular_films rpf ON cbs.customer_country = rpf.country AND rpf.rn = 1\nORDER BY cbs.total_spent DESC, cbs.total_rentals DESC, cbs.customer_name ASC;"}
